[h1]TooltipLib v1.5.0[/h1]
[i]Released: 2026-07-04[/i]

[b]Tooltips become skinnable: consumer mods can now dress the whole tooltip box, declare accent colours and titled sections, and — new in this release — fully take over an item's vanilla rows. Nothing changes visually unless a mod that uses these features is installed.[/b]

[h1]What's New (for players)[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Nothing, by itself.[/b] This release is framework plumbing for mods built on TooltipLib (SauceTooltips, Sauce Inventory Rendering, VorpallySauced). Update it when they ask for it; your tooltips look exactly the same until a consumer mod dresses them.
[/list]

[h1]What's New (for addon authors)[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Panel dress API.[/b] setPanelDress{id, active, draw, surfaces} / clearPanelDress lets a mod paint the tooltip's box (background + frame) on item, object, and world-object surfaces. TooltipLib suppresses the vanilla black box while a dress is active, restores it on error, and yields to foreign tooltip owners.
[*][b]Accent channel.[/b] ctx:setAccentColor(...) on item/itemSlot declares the classic coloured bar as data instead of painted pixels — the framework draws one bar when undressed, and hands the accent to the dress when dressed. Accents now land on the very first frame (no flash).
[*][b]Section channel.[/b] ctx:beginSection(label, color?) titles a block of rows on every surface. Undressed it renders as an uppercased label + divider; a dress with ornaments registered gets precise per-row geometry (rows, kinds, bar fractions, leader lines) reconstructed at declare time — no Java layout reads.
[*][b]Full row takeover.[/b] Item providers may declare replacesVanilla=true to claim an item's vanilla tooltip rows entirely: TooltipLib skips the vanilla embed for that item, draws the name line itself, and the provider re-emits everything through the context API — the whole card becomes dressable. Built for SauceTooltips' VanillaCore.
[*][b]Multi-line labels measure true.[/b] A single addLabel with embedded newlines now spans its real height in ornament geometry (this misaligned dressed cards for mods that pass multi-paragraph labels).
[*][b]Debug probe.[/b] TooltipLib.gd() dumps the last real-pass ornament geometry to the console for dress authors.
[*][b]Deferred-mode dressing, hardened.[/b] Under a foreign tooltip framework (StarlitLibrary-style hosts), TooltipLib appends provider content below the foreign card — a dress can skin that extension (spec.drawDeferred), the classic accent line spans the whole card every frame, foreign extents are remembered per item across hovers (hosts with layout caches no longer flap between appended and plain), full-card claimers stand down instead of duplicating vanilla's rows, and the vanilla box is never suppressed on a panel the dress hasn't proven it owns. Render-replacing mods that size their extra content on the panel (Show Weapon Stats Plus) get covered by the dress too.
[*][b]Consistency setting.[/b] New option: "Keep the tooltip skin alongside other tooltip frameworks" (default OFF). By default, the moment another tooltip framework is detected the skin stands down for the whole session so every tooltip matches; turn it ON for the mixed look (dressed where owned, vanilla where foreign). All information is shown either way.
[/list]

[h1]Compatibility[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Save-safe.[/b] No save-data changes.
[*][b]Backwards compatible.[/b] Every v1.4 provider runs unchanged; all new channels are opt-in. Consumer mods that direct-draw their accent bars keep working (the channel is preferred, not required).
[*][b]Fail-open.[/b] A dress that errors is cleared and the vanilla box returns — a broken skin can never take your tooltips down with it.
[/list]
